.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/ef/efac5abdc5ec66c794f01c53d14b10f51ff527699eae40d2f6d5802f7b51a15e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/76/76182965d505e8c87d15b65569c6a67f9e6e0baedbbf18ece2ff59feb85efd69.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/41cd1216273c3157628ffb6879f50bc41dbed5490b310f5247914b2d47e6500c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ccf6e6d-aaeb-5896-b8d7-bc61aff893e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525992 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/a4/a4ac07da53b80c9ce828e9adca5efc41635d2ca4d56b97340aafa09c824bf956.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/28/28fc05b5ed03768282704609a1d4c05e5c579e75bca0bd5c9f4776c460fe8ae1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d3c459cda9cd4cf2c1cb6b0a84b66108fab84d59a87bd14ed11b6ea80cbf8212.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(4x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1dc940f3-d894-5aef-acf1-d5fe41dbd6a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525993 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fe8a51d1b19bbe18f90b80692df31a01debd536a46569104b1e01a18f2bae87.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4x).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3865abcbb54d17f0af7594c7c50de9301e795b7ac1c364b180e782aa0cf94be.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186